# gen-go.aon --- Go structs from model.aon.
#
# The shape every generator here uses, and why:
#
#   [&: {...}] & $.records     a LIST spread, because list order is
#                              SOURCE order. `pack` keys by data and
#                              map keys sort by code point, so
#                              pack-then-pick would emit the records
#                              alphabetically -- silently wrong output
#                              for a file.
#
#   rows: [&: ...] & .fields   the per-field lines, STAGED into
#                              a key of their own. `pick` over an
#                              inline spread expression does not
#                              resolve (it stays a symbolic call);
#                              staging it into a named sibling makes
#                              it settle. It cannot be hidden -- see
#                              the note below.
#
#   body: pick(.rows, out)     the projection, in source order.
#
# The source record's keys ride through the meet -- `name`, `fields`
# and the rest appear beside `head`/`body`/`tail` in the output. That
# is not sloppiness: a spread MEETS, and the meet is exactly what
# makes `.name` and `.fields` resolvable inside the template. A
# REPLACING combinator would lose the reference (G9, "Two specialist
# disagreements, resolved").
#
# `rows` IS NOT HIDDEN, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. The obvious spelling
# wraps the staged intermediate in `hide()` so the scaffolding stays
# out of the generated value. It does not survive the parity probe:
# inside `hide()` the Go port never resolves the spread template's
# `.n`, so the transform generates in TypeScript and refuses in Go
# with `mapval_no_gen` -- opposite exit codes on the same document
# (use-cases/BUGS.md 63). Left unhidden, both ports agree byte for
# byte, which check.sh asserts. `rows` therefore rides in the
# evaluated value and the fold ignores it.
@"./model.aon"

units: [&: {
  head: `type ` + .name + ` struct {`
  rows: [&: {
    out: `\t` + .go + ` ` +
         match(.t, "string", `string`, "integer", `int64`) +
         ` \`json:"` + .n + `"\``
  }] & .fields
  body: pick(.rows, out)
  tail: `}`
}] & $.records
